York to Texas, famous among epicures. See ''diamond-backed turtle'' (under ''diamond-backed''), and ''Malaclemmys.'' {{smaller|In trade use the sexes are distinguished as ''bull'' and ''cow'', and small ones as ''little bulls'' and ''heifers'' respectively. Those under 5 or 6 inches in total length of the under shell are termed ''cullings'', of which it takes from 18 to 24 or more to make a "dozen." Those of 6 inches and more are ''counts'' or ''counters'', of 12 to the dozen. Only the cows reach 6½ to 7 inches in this measurement; these are known to dealers as ''full counts'', and are especially valuable because they usually contain eggs; the bulls are tougher as well as smaller, and of less market value.}}

'''2.''' Some other tortoise or turtle: as, the elephant ''terrapin'' of the Galapagos.—'''3.''' A dish made of the diamond-back.

{{smaller block|''Terrapin'' is essentially a Philadelphia dish. Baltimore delights in it, Washington eats it, New York knows it, but in Philadelphia it approaches a crime not to be passionately fond of it.
{{right|''J. W. Forney'', The Epicure.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Alligator terrapin.''' See ''alligator-terrapin.''—'''Diamond-backed terrapin,''' the diamond-backed turtle. See ''diamond-backed'', and def. 1.—'''Elephant terrapin.''' See ''elephant tortoise'', under ''tortoise.''—'''Mud-terrapin,''' any mud-turtle, as of the genus ''Cinosternum.'' [U. S.]—'''Painted terrapin''' or '''turtle,''' ''Chrysemys picta'', of the United States. See ''Chrysemys.''—'''Pine-barren terrapin,''' the gopher of the southern United States, ''Testudo carolina.''—'''Red-bel-}}

